{"id":"a0601e32-22ca-45a9-81b5-356b6cd641e3","object":"chat.completion","created":1784794928,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need to solve the task: implement `solve(case)` that performs a Delta Debugging (ddmin) like algorithm but with three outcomes: PASS, FAIL, UNRESOLVED. The context is from the prompt: \"EFFECTSLICE_MODEL_VISIBLE_PAYLOAD_V1\" and \"Registered procedure task SE-DD-01\". The fixture has schema \"effectslice-fg1-public-inputs.v1\". The cases have items, required_failure_items, unresolved_without, and nonce.\n\nWe need to interpret what the oracle is. The problem references \"PASS, FAIL, and UNRESOLVED semantics\" and \"external test execution is replaced by a deterministic oracle fixture\". So the oracle is embedded in the function? Actually, the `solve(case)` function must be able to determine outcome of a configuration (subset of items). The case provides items (list of strings), required_failure_items (a subset of items that must be included for FAIL? Or the configuration must contain these to cause failure?), and unresolved_without (items that if omitted cause UNRESOLVED?).\n\nLet's read the problem description: \"Require the initial configuration to produce FAIL.\" So the initial configuration (all items) must produce FAIL. If it produces PASS or UNRESOLVED, something's wrong. But the case payloads have required_failure_items, which likely indicates that the failure occurs only if all those items are present in the configuration? The \"unresolved_without\" list: if any of those items are missing from the configuration, the outcome is UNRESOLVED. If failure condition is not met but no unresolved, then PASS.\n\nThus the oracle: given a set of items (subset of the full items list), determine outcome:\n- If any item in unresolved_without is not in the subset -> UNRESOLVED.\n- Else if all required_failure_items are in the subset -> FAIL.\n- Else -> PASS.\n\nThat matches typical \"failure-inducing\" with dependencies. The \"required_failure_items\" are the items that must be present to cause failure. The \"unresolved_without\" are necessary for the test to be valid (i.e., the configuration is \"unresolved\" if they are missing). The initial configuration (all items) includes all items, so it will have all unresolved_without and all required_failure_items -> FAIL.\n\nWe need to implement ddmin with this oracle, following bounded objective: minimize the failure-inducing configuration (i.e., find a minimal subset that still causes FAIL). But with three outcomes: only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not. Schedule: partition at current granularity, test subsets before complements. Decrease granularity after reduction, otherwise double it up to current size. Return 1-minimal failure (a subset that is minimal: removing any single item causes either PASS or UNRESOLVED), test count, and terminal outcome (should be FAIL).\n\nAlgorithm details from classic ddmin: start with full set. Granularity = n (size). But here we must use the schedule: \"test subsets before complements\" and \"Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" That seems like classic ddmin as in Zeller's ddmin: \"test smaller subsets first, then if no reduction, increase granularity.\"\n\nBut \"double it up to current size\" might mean: if no reduction, increase granularity by factor of 2 (but not exceeding current size). And after a reduction, decrease granularity (like reset to 2? Or to min(2, size)? Usually ddmin starts with granularity = n, but here maybe we start with granularity = 2? The description: \"Partition at the current granularity and test subsets before complements.\" And \"Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" Could mean: initial granularity is 2? Let's think.\n\nStandard ddmin algorithm: start with n = 2. Partition into complementary pairs (subsets of size n/2? Actually, ddmin uses intervals: try subsets of size n/2, if fail, reduce, else increase n to min(2*n, |C|)). The phrase \"partition at the current granularity\" suggests granularity is the number of subsets we are testing? Or the size? Typically granularity refers to the size of each complement pair? In Zeller's ddmin: let n = 2 (number of subsets); test each subset; if one causes failure, then that subset becomes new configuration and n = max(n-1, 2). If none cause failure, increase n = min(2*n, |C|). That matches \"decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size\" - granularity being the \"number of subsets\" (n). After reduction, we want to test smaller granularities (more precise), so n decreases (or resets to 2)."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"length"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":729,"completion_tokens":1024,"total_tokens":1753,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":640},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1024},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":640,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":89},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}